WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Let's look at security from the AppSec team's
perspective.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.280
We'll start with the deep scan of the
repository,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.020
explore the attack surface, and investigate a
finding by

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.560
following the evidence back to the code.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.400
Then we'll look at how Fix with AI turns

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.280
that finding into a proposed patch.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.740
With test results, the team can inspect before
deciding

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:19.380
what to merge.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.040
We'll start with an application security review
of this

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:24.180
repository.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.080
The question is where customer data could be
exposed

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.980
and what evidence we have to investigate that
risk.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.820
An AI deep scan examines committed code across
the repository.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.820
Here we can choose the repository and review the

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:38.440
scan setup.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.760
This demo already has scan results so I'll close

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.740
the setup and use the existing analysis.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.900
Let's open attack surface.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.860
This gives us a view of the application's
security

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.160
relevant components and how they connect.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.200
We can use it to explore where requests enter,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.980
which controls apply and where sensitive
operations happen.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.960
For this example, we'll focus on invoice search.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.500
A customer signs in, sends a search request and

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.780
receives matching invoices.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.140
The important question is whether those results
stay within

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.240
that customer's tenant.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.060
Authentication is one part of that path.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.860
The database query also needs to enforce the
boundary

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:17.780
between the customers.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.180
I'll use the map and its supporting evidence to

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.140
examine how the request reaches that query.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.440
The scan results describe the code that was
analyzed.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.460
As we investigate, we need to keep that scope

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.440
in mind, especially if the repository has
changed since

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:33.880
the scan.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.640
Now let's open findings for this repository and
select

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.820
the SQL injection finding.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.360
It's marked high severity and open.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.240
The affected code is an invoice search.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.320
The finding also shows reachability and
exploitability, which helps

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.920
us prioritize the investigation.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:53.760
Here's the evidence.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.720
Search text comes from authenticated invoice
search request.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.500
The application passes that text and the session
user

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.840
into the search function.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.360
The function then inserts both values directly
into a

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:06.860
SQL command.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.220
A crafted search can change the command and
bypass

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.000
the tenant condition.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.740
In this demo, that could expose an invoice
belonging

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.980
to another customer, which even though the
request came

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.000
from a signed-in user.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.700
I can open the cited lines and inspect the

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:22.960
query.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.640
The finding includes steps to reproduce the
issue using

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.720
the demo’s seeded invoices, along with the
recommendation

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.300
to bind the tenant ID and search text as

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:32.580
parameters.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.020
That means passing the values separately from
the SQL

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.460
command, so the database treats them as data.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.840
Next is Fix with AI.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.100
This can turn the finding and its supporting
context

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.140
into a coding task for a proposed fix.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.840
We've already completed the task for this
finding, so

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.520
the action now says view PR.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.180
Let's open the proposed change and look at the

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:55.420
result.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.660
The patch replaces the interpolated values with
query parameters.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.960
The customer isolation test also changes from an
expected

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.820
failure to a normal regression test.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.780
The coding task reports that the injection
attempt no

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.600
longer returns another customer's invoices,
while a normal search

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:14.220
still works.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:18.760
It reports 57 passing tests with 5 unrelated
expected

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:19.200
failures.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.420
Those results give us something concrete to
review.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.300
We still need to inspect the patch and the

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.780
checks before accepting the change.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.100
For this demo, we'll leave the proposed fix
unmerged.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.620
We've gone from a repository scan to the
application's

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.580
attack surface, followed a finding back to its
code,

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.140
and reviewed a proposed fix.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.000
That's the AppSec workflow we are showing here.
